# Argdown+ Primer

[?Q-UBI]: Should UBI be introduced nationwide?

[S-Poverty]: UBI reduces poverty rates.

[A-PovertyModel]: Poverty floor argument.
  +> [S-Poverty]

[S-Poverty]
  !> [?Q-UBI]: Yes, direct transfers reduce poverty.
  => [S-LowerMaterialDeprivation]: Lower deprivation follows from lower poverty.
  :> [S-ChildPoverty]: Effect is strongest for child poverty.
  ~= [S-MaterialSecurity]: The claim is close in meaning to improved material security.

[S-ChildPoverty]: Child poverty drops when transfer floors exist.

[S-LowerMaterialDeprivation]: Fewer households lack essentials.

[S-MaterialSecurity]: Households gain greater material security.

[?Q-UBI]
  ?> [S-Poverty]

[@R-Meta]: https://doi.org/10.1038/s41562-019-1458-5

[>E-Meta] >>
    "Cash transfers significantly improve household resilience."

[S-Poverty]
  <@ [>E-Meta]: Cash transfers significantly improve household resilience.

[S-StocktonPilot]: Stockton's SEED pilot used direct cash transfers.

[S-StocktonPilot]
  %> [S-Poverty]: A concrete pilot illustrates the broader poverty-reduction claim.
